  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry and comfortable weather is expected \nfor the rest of the weekend. A few showers possible tonight however.\n\nA drier and cooler airmass has arrived across the northern\nMiddle Atlantic region as high pressure to the northwest begins\ninfluencing our weather to a greater degree. Low pressure aloft\nacross the the Northern Atlantic continues to bring Northwest\nflow aloft over our area. A couple embedded short waves in this\nflow will pass across New England and our coastal waters later\ntoday and tonight. The activity will drift further south across\nDelmarva Sunday morning. We could see some scattered showers as\na result. Pops are generally in the 20% to 35% range, so no \nsignificant impacts are expected. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor tidal flooding is anticipated with Sunday \nafternoon's high tide, mainly for the Jersey Shore, Delaware \nBeaches, and Delaware Bay communities.\n\nFlow will turn more onshore over the next day or so. This will \nresult in water piling up over consecutive tide cycles, potentially \nleading to minor tidal flooding across portions of our area on \nSunday afternoon. The current forecast has minor tidal flooding from \nBarnegat Bay on south along the Atlantic Ocean coastline and for \nareas within Delaware Bay. Advisories may be needed in these spots \nbut will wait to see how things trend over the 12 hours with the \nforecast.\n\nWithin the tidal Delaware River, northern NJ coast, and Chesapeake \nBay, tidal flooding will be limited to spotty minor or no flooding \nat all. Tide levels should decrease getting into next week as flow \nturns more offshore.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front will pass through on Thursday bringing \nthe potential for another round of showers and storms.\n\nBy Tuesday, high pressure will begin to shift offshore welcoming the \nreturn of southerly flow, resulting in a period of above-normal\ntemperatures and an increase in humidity levels through mid-week.\nHigh pressure looks like it will largely remain in control\nthrough Wednesday, before giving way to a cold front that will\napproach the area on Thursday. This front will be the next \nopportunity for any showers or thunderstorms around the area. \nHowever, moisture with the front could end up being limited so \nat this point POPs are only around 20-30% or less.",
